INT. GRANDMOTHER'S ATTIC - EVENING

Slanted beams. Trunks, a dressmaker's dummy, a rocking horse with one eye. A single round window.

GRANDMA ODILE, seventies, in a flowered housecoat, sits on a trunk with a shoebox on her knees. DAD MWANGI, forties, crouches by a stack of framed photos, wiping dust.

A BOY, eight, in a too-big pajama top, climbs the attic ladder and peeks over the edge.

BOY
Are you guys up here?

GRANDMA ODILE
Come, sweet pea.

He climbs the rest of the way. Pads across the floor.

BOY
What's in the box?

GRANDMA ODILE
Letters.

BOY
From who?

GRANDMA ODILE
From your grandfather. Before he was your grandfather.

DAD MWANGI
Theo, you should be in bed.

THEO
Grandma's telling a story.

GRANDMA ODILE
(to Mwangi)
Let him stay.

Mwangi sighs. Sits.

THEO
Grandma, are the letters sad?

GRANDMA ODILE
Some are. Some are silly. This one is about a goat.

THEO
A goat!

She unfolds the page. Mwangi smiles despite himself.
